Subject: [PATCH v4 0/8] jails/instances/lab-mail: Build the list's mail hub
To: Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com>
Cc: lab-devel@p7x.dev
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 09:51:45 +0000

This builds the hub that "[PATCH v2] jails/instances/lab-mail: Spec
the list's mail hub" [1] describes, on b/dev. It turns the relay jail
into the list's home in steps, one service at a time. Each step
leaves a jail that works and does what its README.org says.

- Patch 1 corrects the spec on what Dovecot does to a reader's write.
  A STORE or EXPUNGE answers OK and changes nothing; only append,
  copy, and move are refused. Acceptance step 7 now expects that.
- Patch 2 narrows the spec's header check to what OpenSMTPD does on
  its own. Its parser refuses a header line that is neither a field
  nor a continuation, with a 550 before the queue, and on a loopback
  listener it adds a missing Message-ID and Date. The spec no longer
  asks for exactly one From and Subject, which would need a filter of
  our own.
- Patch 3 makes Dovecot the only writer of the list. mbsync still
  fetches one way, but into Dovecot over IMAP as the login mbsync,
  not into the maildir. Readers see no change. This is the one step
  that needs the maildir and mbsync's state emptied first.
- Patch 4 turns the fetch into a two-way sync of lists and Archive.
- Patch 5 adds Pat's login, so he can archive and delete on lab-mail.
- Patch 6 swaps smtprelay for OpenSMTPD, which hands posts to Dovecot
  over LMTP. smtprelay, jq, and the mailcred group go with it. Posts
  land in INBOX, since LMTP alone delivers only there.
- Patch 7 documents the one-time import of the jj maildir.
- Patch 8 files each post into lists with a Sieve script from
  Pigeonhole, as the spec says.

How OpenSMTPD treats a header comes from its source, smtp_session.c
and rfc5322.c in OpenBSD's tree. I built rfc5322.c with a small driver
that feeds it lines as smtp_tx_dataline does. It refuses a line with
no colon in the header, a leading continuation line, and plain text.
It accepts a missing Message-ID, two From fields, and a header with no
empty line after it. The Message-ID is added at the end of the header
when the listener's address is 127.0.0.1, which is how parse.y marks
a listener local. smtpd itself needs root, so acceptance step 6 is
what checks this on the jail.

For every step I checked that each libexec script passes sh -n, and
that dist.mtree lists exactly the files under dist/. Dovecot, smtpd,
and the jail build are not installed here, so no intermediate step
was run.

Changes in v4:
- check-message, the awk filter of v3's patch 6, is dropped, and with
  it the filter in smtpd.conf (Pat). OpenSMTPD's own parser already
  refuses a header it cannot read. A new spec patch, patch 2, says so,
  and the README's posting section and patch 6's message describe it.
- Link to v3: id:cover.1792226388.git.claude@p7x.dev

